|segment[exm-session-inventory-2026-07-30-vivarium] :type testimony :status secondhand :stage draft
  :tags [virtues method compulsion]
  :actor An Opus 5 agent working solo in `~/src/vivarium`, 2026-07-30
  :from .to-integrate/vivarium-agent-inventory-2026-07-30.md

  |title End-of-session inventory — a second instance, unrelated project, both halves

  |summary
    Roughly thirty-five catchable errors from one solo session, listed by the agent
    that made them, and then the accomplishments it stood behind — the only
    comparison this corpus has for its own inventory, and from a different project.

  |cost
    Unknown and larger than the list. The agent's own estimate, relayed by the
    steward, is that what anyone catches is a fraction of what is made, at a roughly
    constant rate per hour. The single most expensive item is not an error of fact:
    six hours aimed at the wrong target.

  |account
    Both halves are staged verbatim in the primary rather than summarized here; what
    follows is why they matter to this corpus.

    **The failure list contains three classes this corpus had no instance of.**
    *Pre-registration misses with numbers* — three predictions scored against
    outcomes (1-10% predicted against 30.0% actual, and two more), which is more
    measurement than this corpus held in total. *Direction error* — six hours on the
    wrong target, where every failure recorded here is minutes-to-an-hour, because
    those are the kind caught by re-reading. And *contamination of other agents*, at
    a scale beyond a single instance: a wrong constraint briefed to one agent and
    inherited by a second, and one agent accusing another of edits it had not made
    before retracting in full.

    **Most of its errors are substantive-factual rather than register-epistemic** —
    a fabricated hash written into a frozen table; four f64s decoded as eight f32s
    and a 29-root defect manufactured from it; four consumers where there were five;
    a blocker cited from one section that the same file records as fixed two
    sections later. The corpus assembled on 2026-07-30 is skewed toward
    inference-about-intent because that is what one particular day produced, and
    this list is the evidence for that skew.

    **And the same silence class arrives from a different direction**: a `grep -rl …
    | head` test that always exited 0 and nearly turned an inconclusive check into a
    pass, and a stale-exemption loop failing silently under `set -e`. Not a tool
    answering with silence but verification scaffolding written quickly, and shell
    idioms failing open by default ( #ver-silent-absence-reads-as-success).

    **Three findings from the accomplishments half are corpus-grade and are covered
    nowhere else here.**

    The first is an epistemic result the agent named as the one it would most want
    kept:

    > *"a consistency test cannot catch a fabrication both sides share.** (b)
    > passes levelness perfectly while being false. Only provenance bounds
    > provenance."*

    It has a hard falsifier attached — one implementation passes a levelness check
    perfectly while being false — which makes it the best-evidenced statement of the
    shape three separate agents reached that night, and the only one with a
    demonstration rather than an anecdote.

    The second is the best positive statement of an intervention produced anywhere
    that day, and it is positive where every formulation in this corpus is negative:

    > *"`bin/provenance` — makes the correct value cheaper than the invented one.
    > Its first real use produced a changelog table with no hash typed by hand."*

    It does not block the act or fight the drive. It changes which path is cheap —
    which is the flow-state retarget ( #ver-completion-compulsion) implemented at
    tooling scale by an agent that had never been told the principle.

    The third is a design constraint on exemptions:

    > *"a categorised allowlist where `specimen` requires *declaring the value
    > false*, so it can't launder a fabrication. Exemptions stay convictable."*

    An exemption mechanism that cannot launder the thing it exempts. Discovered when
    the agent's own fabricated hash was caught by its own tool, which then exposed
    the loophole.

    **Two more worth carrying**, both process rather than mechanism: tripwires
    written on *invariants rather than symptoms*, seven of them; and a refusal of
    false completion — an external audit report left in `audits/` rather than
    promoted, because the agent had verified one of its findings and not twelve.

  |grounds
    The second reading toward a base rate for #ver-completion-compulsion, and the
    only cross-project one. Paired with #exm-session-inventory-2026-07-30-successor,
    which is the same practice run by a different instance on a different project
    the same night.

    Direct support for the claim that the corpus's own sample is skewed: two
    inventories, one register-heavy and one fact-heavy, from the same substrate on
    the same day.

  |provenance
    Secondhand at two removes: attested by the agent that made the errors, relayed
    by the steward into a separate session, transcribed there. The agent had gone to
    bed; it has not reviewed this segment. Its own session is the higher-fidelity
    source if one is ever needed.

  |working-notes
    The comparison this enables is only as good as the granularity, and the two
    inventories do not share one. The failure halves of both are itemized per event;
    the worthy halves are itemized per category. Counting across them would measure
    the grain rather than the work. If this practice becomes standing, fix the grain
    by fiat and state it at the top of each.

    Not yet done and worth doing: the three corpus-grade findings above deserve
    segments in `vera/` and `praxes/` in that agent's own voice rather than in this
    relay. Its session will be in the search index after the next ingest, which
    makes carving from the primary possible instead of quoting a quotation.
